sections = [
('1 Architecture comparison and justification', [
('1.1 Applying the two architectures', '''NWSDB needs a consistent way for its website and external partners to retrieve balances, record payments and obtain recent usage. WaterLink addresses this through independently hosted usage and payment services, consumed by an ASP.NET Core web portal and a .NET desktop payment client. The central design concern is preserving a trustworthy payment record while allowing different interfaces to evolve. This report evaluates the architecture, explains the implemented application, presents observed testing evidence and assesses deployment choices.

A monolithic implementation would place account screens, meter processing, payment rules and partner endpoints inside one deployable application. Its modules could still follow good interfaces and dependency injection; monolithic does not mean unstructured. The website and partners could call its HTTP endpoints. One database transaction could cover related changes, and a small team would have fewer hosts to configure, monitor and release. This makes a modular monolith a credible low-cost starting point.

However, a release that changes meter processing would normally deploy the same application that accepts payments. A failure or resource-intensive operation could affect both capabilities. When payment requests rise near a billing deadline, replicating the whole application also replicates unrelated functionality. A monolith can scale horizontally, but the deployment unit is larger and its shared database can remain a bottleneck. These disadvantages concern the proposed deployment structure, not an assertion that every monolith is slow.

SOA exposes reusable business capabilities through documented service interfaces. IBM (n.d.) describes interoperability and loose coupling as central characteristics, and recognises both SOAP and RESTful HTTP. Here, payment recording is available to the web portal and desktop partner application without duplicating financial rules. The usage service owns meter readings; the payment service owns bills and receipts. Neither client opens a database. This arrangement directly supports NWSDB's requirement to cross platform and organisational boundaries.'''),
('1.2 Maintainability scalability and trade offs', '''Maintainability improves when a change has a clear owner. For example, changing meter precision primarily affects the usage service and its tests. Altering receipt presentation affects a client, while duplicate-payment protection remains in the payment repository. Public message contracts are separated from persistence code. Versioned URLs and documented response semantics allow a service implementation to change while consumers continue using the same contract. Breaking changes would still require an explicit migration period; independence is not automatic.

For scalability, payment and usage hosts form separate deployment units. A future server-database implementation could add payment instances during collection peaks without increasing usage instances. Microsoft (n.d.a) associates independent deployment and scaling with autonomous services, while warning about distributed-system complexity. WaterLink applies those techniques to its service boundaries; it does not equate all SOA with microservices. Its current SQLite databases support the local prototype, not unrestricted multi-host replication. Database migration and shared identity configuration are prerequisites for that production scaling claim.

The costs are additional network calls, authentication between components, deployment coordination and partial failure. A healthy portal cannot manufacture a balance if its payment API is unavailable. Independent databases also make cross-service transactions harder. WaterLink avoids a distributed payment transaction by keeping the bill and receipt ledger together. Meter updates do not immediately calculate a new charge, because an official tariff and billing integration are outside this prototype. The following comparison therefore evaluates concrete responsibilities rather than generic architectural slogans.

SOA is the stronger fit because NWSDB explicitly needs reusable capabilities for multiple external consumers. Separate service ownership enables focused maintenance and provides a practical route to selective scaling. A modular monolith would remain reasonable for a small internal-only application, but repeated partner-specific logic would undermine this case's integration objective. The recommended architecture accepts operational complexity in exchange for reusable interfaces, while keeping only two business services to avoid unnecessary fragmentation.''')]),
('2 Application design and development', [
('2.1 Scope and service boundaries', '''WaterLink implements account balance lookup, payment recording, receipt history, usage retrieval and operator-entered meter readings. Customers access their own account; a partner can look up balances and record collections; the operator supplies the restricted administrative function of updating readings. The two seeded customer records and fixed bills are fictional. Payments demonstrate ledger updates without transferring money. Self-registration, real bank settlement, email delivery and official tariff calculation are identified as future integrations rather than presented as finished features.

The deployment diagram shows actual hosts and communication paths. Water.Portal serves the website and forwards authorised HTTP requests. Water.Usage and Water.Payments are separate ASP.NET Core processes with separate SQLite files. Water.Desktop is a second .NET consumer using Avalonia for its interface. The web client uses JavaScript and JSON, demonstrating that consumers need not share the service implementation language. REST/JSON was selected for straightforward browser interoperability; the written requirement specifies a .NET API without mandating SOAP or WCF.

Water.Contracts contains reusable payment message records. Water.Shared supplies validation, identity helpers and database connection utilities. Each API injects a repository interface whose implementation owns SQL access. This separates transport, business validation and persistence concerns without introducing an unnecessary enterprise service bus. Microsoft (n.d.b) recommends platform-independent interfaces and loose coupling; the versioned resources follow that guidance. Independent organisations would receive their own credentials and contractual access scopes in a production onboarding process.'''),
('2.2 Data integrity and payment flow', '''The data model places Accounts and Readings in the usage database, and Bills and Payments in the payment database. Account identifiers are integration keys across services, not cross-database foreign keys. Within each database, foreign keys protect ownership relationships. A bill holds the demonstration amount; its outstanding balance is derived from billed cents minus recorded payment cents. This avoids maintaining a second mutable balance that could drift from the ledger. History is bounded to the latest 100 receipts and 12 meter readings.

Amounts arrive as decimal values and are validated before conversion to integer cents. Zero, negative, excessive and fractional-cent amounts are rejected. Meter values use integer thousandths of a cubic metre in storage. A new cumulative reading must not decrease, must not exceed supported precision, and must have a timestamp later than the previous reading but no later than current UTC. Meter replacement requires a separate future workflow; silently accepting a lower reading would create misleading consumption.

A payment begins an immediate database transaction before reading its available balance. The repository first checks the unique combination of channel and reference. A matching account and amount returns the original receipt; a changed payload produces a conflict. Only a genuinely new, affordable payment is inserted and committed. This ordering permits a retry even when the original payment already settled the bill. Microsoft (n.d.c) explains SQLite's atomic transactions and single-writer constraint, which inform this design.

The payment flow diagram identifies replay and overpayment outcomes. With two competing full-balance payments, one transaction can commit and the other must re-evaluate the remaining balance before acceptance. A unique database constraint also guards the reference pair. The approach protects this local ledger from duplicate requests, but it does not prove exactly-once settlement across a bank network. A real payment provider would additionally require verified settlement notifications, reconciliation and recovery procedures.'''),
('2.3 API contracts security and reuse', '''The service contract defines HTTP methods, request schemas, roles and status codes. Successful new payments return 201; matching replays return 200. Invalid values return 400, unauthenticated callers 401, forbidden access 403, missing accounts 404 and business conflicts 409. Unexpected failures return a generic problem response while server logs retain a correlation identifier. The accompanying API guide and OpenAPI document make these conventions available to another consumer. Microsoft (n.d.d) identifies idempotency as a resilience strategy for safe retries.

Authentication and authorisation are enforced in the services, not merely through hidden buttons. A customer requesting another account receives 403. Partners cannot read personal usage and see only their own channel's receipts. Operators cannot record payments. The browser authenticates through an HTTP-only, SameSite Strict cookie; the portal holds service credentials on the server. POST requests require a custom header and cross-origin access is not enabled. Login attempts are limited to ten per fixed minute across the demonstration portal.

These measures are appropriate for a local assessment prototype, but its fixed identities and shared demonstration password are deliberately limited. Production should use an identity provider, per-partner credentials, credential rotation and more granular throttling. The HTTPS cookie setting is enabled outside Development. No real card details are collected. Parameterised SQL prevents user input from becoming executable query text, and browser rendering uses textContent rather than inserting returned values as HTML.

The following excerpts are extracted directly from the delivered source. The first isolates reusable money validation; the second shows how the desktop client consumes the payment endpoint. The source archive supplies complete implementations rather than relying on these shortened report excerpts.'''),
('2.4 Client experience and maintainability', '''The web dashboard prioritises outstanding balance, recorded consumption and received payments. Receipt history is adjacent to the payment form, allowing the customer to connect an action with its result. After acceptance, the portal refreshes the balance and activity list. A retained reference supports retry after an error; successful submission creates a new reference for the next payment. Forms provide labels, validation, disabled submission controls during requests and an accessible status message. Desktop, tablet and mobile layouts were checked in Chromium.

Usage is described as the difference between the latest two recorded cumulative readings. The interface shows when the last reading was recorded and when the balance was checked. It does not label historical meter data as live telemetry. Data is fetched on login, explicit refresh and successful operations; no background push mechanism is claimed. Partial service failures leave unavailable values blank and display an error rather than presenting an old balance as current.

The desktop partner interface provides account lookup, amount entry, a unique reference and receipt feedback. Its PaymentClient uses the public contracts and HTTP only. The same service-side validation therefore applies to both consumers. Its automated smoke mode exercises this actual client class against a running API; native desktop visual verification remains a separate limitation. Repository interfaces, named business records, nullable checking, warnings-as-errors and bounded HTTP timeouts support future maintenance.

The source package includes build and run scripts, API documentation, editable diagrams, deployment files and reproducible tests. Local data and port settings can be changed without editing business code. Tests should use a new data directory because integration checks deliberately settle one seeded account. Before public deployment, schema migration, account provisioning and operational monitoring should be introduced. These are explicit extension points, not unsupported claims about present functionality.''')]),
('3 Testing debugging and results', [
('3.1 Test strategy and observed results', '''Verification combined focused validation checks, real HTTP integration tests, browser workflows and a desktop HTTP-client smoke test. Sixteen unit assertions cover exact cents, minimum and maximum values, reference validation, timestamp ordering and meter precision. Twenty-six integration checks exercise running service hosts and SQLite persistence, including account isolation, role restrictions, duplicate requests, overpayments and competing transactions. These are executable assertions with saved results; they are not a claimed percentage of code coverage.

Ten browser checks cover invalid login, dashboard loading, a recorded payment, role-specific controls, browser exceptions and horizontal overflow at 768 and 390 pixels. A desktop-width browser screenshot was also captured at 1440 pixels. Four desktop-client checks confirm balance retrieval, payment acceptance, identical receipt on replay and the updated balance. The combined result is 56 passing checks. The final solution build completed with zero warnings and zero errors. Table 3 separates these categories so their scope is auditable.

The concurrent full-payment test observed HTTP 201 and 409, followed by a zero outstanding balance. Concurrent duplicate requests returned 201 and 200 with the same receipt identifier. These outcomes support the implemented transaction and idempotency rules. They do not establish peak production throughput, distributed failover or every possible race. Integration and browser tests were repeated against an isolated data directory after the final service build; the main demonstration data was preserved.'''),
('3.2 Debugging and remaining validation', '''The first package restore failed because the initial SQLite dependency chain triggered a high-severity vulnerability warning. With warnings treated as errors, this blocked progress visibly. Updating Microsoft.Data.Sqlite to 10.0.12 resolved restore without disabling vulnerability auditing. A second reproducible build failure came from Avalonia's obsolete TextBox.Watermark property. The preserved compiler output identified the affected lines; replacing it with PlaceholderText produced a successful rebuild. These are observed faults and corrections rather than invented debugging stories.

Review also identified that the container's non-root user needed a writable data directory. The Dockerfile now creates and assigns that directory before switching user. Login rate limiting and a separate public-contract library were added during the same review. Native desktop mouse and keyboard behaviour, Windows compatibility, Docker execution, Kubernetes deployment, real banking integration and sustained load remain unverified. The application should be demonstrated with the saved tests and a fresh local run; those limits should remain visible when interpreting its readiness.''')]),
('4 Deployment techniques and recommendation', [
('4.1 Server containers and orchestration', '''A conventional server can run the published ASP.NET Core hosts under a service manager behind a TLS reverse proxy. This has relatively low operational overhead and suits an initial controlled deployment. The operator must configure startup, certificates, logs, secret storage and backups. Separate service processes preserve independent release boundaries even when they share one machine. That machine remains a common failure point, so availability depends on its recovery arrangements.

Docker packages each host with a consistent runtime, reducing differences between development and deployment. The supplied multi-stage Dockerfile builds a selected service and runs it as a non-root user. Compose defines the portal, two private APIs and independent persistent volumes. Docker (n.d.) documents production-specific Compose configuration, including restart policies and environment changes. The provided file is a loopback demonstration configuration: public deployment requires HTTPS, production settings, secured secrets and persistent Data Protection keys.

Kubernetes adds scheduling, rollout control and service replication, but also demands cluster administration, monitoring and resource planning. Its production guidance stresses availability, scale and operational responsibility (Kubernetes, n.d.). For WaterLink, replicas must not each maintain an independent SQLite payment ledger. Before horizontal replication, each business service needs an appropriate server database, consistent identity configuration and tested concurrency behaviour. Kubernetes cannot repair an incorrectly partitioned financial record merely by restarting containers.'''),
('4.2 Recommended deployment path', '''For the assessment, the verified local process deployment is the reproducible choice. For a small pilot, Docker Compose on a managed server offers a practical balance of portability, maintenance effort and cost, once its configuration is exercised and secured. Kubernetes becomes justified only when measured traffic, availability objectives and an operations team support its additional complexity. No monetary saving or capacity figure is invented because the case provides no workload measurements or hosting budget.

A release pipeline should restore audited dependencies, build, run isolated tests, publish images and verify a staging environment before rollout. Database changes need backward-compatible migrations and rollback planning. SQLite backups must use its online backup facility or stop writers; copying a live database file alone can omit write-ahead-log changes. Restore drills, correlation-aware logs and database readiness checks are necessary operational additions. These recommendations distinguish a working local SOC demonstration from a system ready to receive real public payments.''')])]
conclusion='''WaterLink demonstrates reusable .NET services consumed by distinct web and desktop clients. Its strongest architectural choice is keeping financial consistency inside one payment boundary while separating usage responsibility. Recorded tests support its local payment, authorisation and usage behaviour. A production system would retain those contracts while replacing demonstration identities, integrating settlement and billing systems, and validating the chosen hosting environment.'''
